{"step_index": 1, "thought": "Action: Finish", "action": "Finish", "action_input": "Safety Risks for IMS Equipment: - Electrical hazards (shock, arc\u2011flash) due to faulty wiring, poor grounding, or degraded insulation. - Power loss or UPS failure leading to loss of safety\u2011critical monitoring and control functions. - Software/firmware defects causing incorrect alarm generation, loss of data integrity, or unintended system behavior. - Cyber\u2011security breaches (unauthorized access, malware) that could compromise safety functions or tamper with data. - Sensor drift, data corruption, or communication failures resulting in inaccurate condition monitoring and false safety decisions. - Human\u2011machine interface errors (mis\u2011configuration, operator misunderstanding) that can lead to unsafe actions. - Environmental exposure (temperature, humidity, vibration) degrading hardware reliability.  Compliance Issues for IMS Equipment: - IEC\u202f61508/IEC\u202f61511 functional safety standards \u2013 need to demonstrate safety integrity level (SIL) compliance for safety\u2011related functions. - IEC\u202f62443 (Industrial Automation and Control Systems Security) \u2013 requirements for cybersecurity risk assessment, hardening, and ongoing monitoring. - ISO\u202f45001 occupational health & safety \u2013 ensuring safe work practices around IMS hardware and maintenance activities. - ISO\u202f9001 quality management \u2013 documentation, change control, and validation of software/firmware updates. - OSHA regulations (e.g., electrical safety, lockout/tagout) \u2013 must meet electrical safety and equipment maintenance standards. - Industry\u2011specific standards (e.g., API 1165 for offshore platforms, FAA/DO\u2011160 for aerospace) if IMS is used in those sectors. - Data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA", "observation": null, "harness_observation": "", "thought_logprobs": [0.0, 0.0, -2.0265599687263602e-06, -0.5761849880218506, -0.016772674396634102, -0.005354